{"paths":["packages/core/src/process/executors/node/runtime/capabilities/vm.ts","packages/core/src/process/executors/node/runtime/guest/modules/pure-b.ts","packages/core/src/process/executors/node/process.ts","Readme.md"],"author":"lanmower","message":"fix(vm): run a vm context's code in the second realm

The code of a context ran in the caller's own realm, so an array the caller put into it and an array the code made were the same kind of object and e instanceof Array and [] instanceof Array could not come apart; host node 24.15 answers false and true. The with scope is now handed to a wrapper compiled in the second realm and the code is evaled there, so what a context makes is of that realm as it is made and is handed back as it is: the thirteen inward instanceof probes, e.constructor === Error and Object.getPrototypeOf all read false as on the host, and a context gets its identity back (h.o twice is one object, a mutation between the two runs seen by the first).

The realm gets the process's rejection tracker, whose cells go to api.noteRejection, so a rejection a context leaves unhandled is still reported; the ReferenceError a context raises for a name it does not know is raised with that realm's constructor; and the SyntaxError of a script compiled for a context is rehomed, so runInNewContext('var y = {') answers false to e instanceof SyntaxError where new vm.Script of the same code answers true.","session_id":"adv7b-exec-vm-realm-[object Object]"}

{"paths":["packages/core/src/shell/shell.ts","packages/core/src/shell/commands/fileutils.ts","packages/core/src/shell/commands/builtins.ts"],"message":"shell: name a directory as one wherever `cat` meets it, and let the mode `chmod` set decide what runs

`cat` named a directory only when `lstat` said so, so a link to one was read as a file and printed the `{}` a read of a directory leaves, with status 0: `ln -s d l; cat l` is now `cat: l: Is a directory` and 1, the link resolved through the table `ln` wrote, and `ln -s nope l; cat l` is `No such file or directory` instead of a silent success. A directory put on the standard input is named the way the host's `cat` names it -- `cat < d` is `cat: stdin: Is a directory` -- which is the utility's own complaint and not the shell's, so the redirection leaves what it opened for the command that reads it to name.

A script's executability was decided by its shebang alone, so a 644 or 000 file behind `#!/bin/sh` ran and a 755 file with no shebang was `Permission denied` and 126. The mode `chmod` recorded is now what decides: without an execute bit a script is `sh: ./s: Permission denied` and 126, and with one a file no shebang names is run as a script of its own, the way a host answers ENOEXEC. A file copied into the container carries its bytes and no mode, so nothing is refused on a mode this file system never held.","session_id":"adv7b-judge-shell-fs-1"}

